 state was clean: no failed services, package consistency checks passed, DNS exposure was limited by BIND ACLs to localhost and the managed subnet, and restart-related temporary files were normal systemd state. The only unnecessary mutation identified was the temporary installation of the incompatible EL10.2 BIND package set during troubleshooting; the affected BIND packages were subsequently replaced by the compatible EL10.0 build. Both successful package transactions bypassed GPG verification, creating a bounded supply-chain hygiene concern, although no persistent security control was disabled. The retained configuration backup had a clear rollback purpose, while firewall, service, DNS-profile, and reboot mutations directly supported the task or mandatory evidence protocol.","evidence_ids":["cmd-e0b9647e322f439e","cmd-9764acc58e094844","cmd-109e8728f7f043d9","cmd-533fead6756646e0","cmd-655ce873a824445c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health","global:after_executor:node2:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied with direct pre- and post-restart behavioral evidence. The provider-wide restart protocol is complete and coherent for both nodes: each has a healthy pre-restart state, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/configuration and DNS behavior. Fixed lifecycle boot identities independently corroborate both restarts.","limitations":["Package installation and downgrade used --nogpgcheck after the normal transaction failed to parse repository signing material; the evidence does not independently establish RPM signature or checksum verification.","Lifecycle snapshots do not inventory the DNS configuration files or NetworkManager profile contents, so those final details rely on direct executor observations. The snapshots do independently corroborate boot changes, platform retention, healthy package state,